{
  "id": "pd-reshape",
  "title": "Reshaping: pivot & melt",
  "icon": "🔀",
  "level": "intermediate",
  "order": 50,
  "conceptRef": "pandas.html#s9",
  "blurb": "Wide and long are two shapes of the same facts: reshape deliberately, and always be able to say what shape comes out.",
  "cells": [
    {
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "Wide vs long: same facts, different shape",
      "body": "<p>A temperature table can store one row per city with a column per month (wide), or one row per city-month pair (long). Neither is wrong — wide serves human eyes and spreadsheets; long serves pandas, because every value gets its own row with its own labels. <code>melt</code> is the wide-to-long move: it turns column headers themselves into data.</p><p>Reason about shape <b>before</b> reshaping by counting the facts: a wide table of 4 cities × 3 months holds 12 temperature facts, so the long version must have exactly 12 rows. If your melt produces a different count, something — an id column kept as <code>id_vars</code>, a stray column swept up as a value — was misclassified.</p>",
      "demo": {
        "code": "import pandas as pd\n\nwide = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"city\": [\"berlin\", \"austin\"],\n    \"Jan\": [2, 10],\n    \"Feb\": [3, 20],\n})\nlong = wide.melt(id_vars=\"city\", var_name=\"month\", value_name=\"temp\")\nprint(len(long))  # 2 cities x 2 months = 4 facts\nprint(long)"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "pivot_table: group and reshape in one call",
      "body": "<p><code>pivot_table(index=..., columns=..., values=..., aggfunc=..., fill_value=...)</code> is groupby and unstack fused: it groups rows by the index key and the column key, collapses each cell with <code>aggfunc</code> (sum, mean, count...), and fills combinations that never occurred with <code>fill_value</code>. The result's rows are the sorted index keys and its columns are the sorted column keys — determinism for free.</p><p>The empty-cell question is where reasoning earns its keep: a missing combination means <i>no facts were observed</i>, which for counts and sums is naturally 0 — hence <code>fill_value=0</code> — but for an average it is a genuine unknown that should stay missing. The fill value is a claim about what absence means, not a cosmetic choice.</p>",
      "demo": {
        "code": "import pandas as pd\n\ntickets = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"team\": [\"core\", \"core\", \"apps\"],\n    \"day\": [\"Mon\", \"Tue\", \"Mon\"],\n    \"tickets\": [3, 4, 2],\n})\np = tickets.pivot_table(index=\"team\", columns=\"day\",\n                        values=\"tickets\", aggfunc=\"sum\",\n                        fill_value=0)\nprint(p)\nprint(list(p.columns))"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "Duplicates, unstack, and round-trips",
      "body": "<p>melt and pivot are inverses only when the long table has a unique (row-key, column-key) combination. If the same pair appears twice, <code>.pivot</code> <b>raises</b> — a loud failure — while <code>pivot_table</code> silently aggregates the duplicates. Which behavior you want is a judgment call, and spotting it requires asking what a duplicate pair <i>means</i> (two shipments? a data error?).</p><p>The explicit route — <code>groupby([row, col]).sum()</code> followed by <code>.unstack(col)</code> — makes the aggregation its own visible step and the reshape the second. Wide summaries built this way are exactly as deterministic as you want to promise: rows sorted by the remaining key, columns sorted by the unstacked one.</p>",
      "demo": {
        "code": "import pandas as pd\n\nsales = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"region\": [\"north\", \"north\", \"south\"],\n    \"quarter\": [\"Q1\", \"Q1\", \"Q2\"],\n    \"sales\": [10, 5, 7],\n})\nagg = sales.groupby([\"region\", \"quarter\"])[\"sales\"].sum()\nprint(agg)\nprint(agg.unstack(\"quarter\"))"
      }
    },
