        "hints": [
          "Count the facts first: how many rows must the long table have? Which column is an identifier (stays) and which columns are really values wearing header hats?",
          "wide.melt(id_vars='city', var_name='month', value_name='temp') turns month headers into rows — but melt emits month-major blocks (all cities for Jan, then Feb...), so a sort is needed afterwards.",
          "long = wide.melt(id_vars='city', var_name='month', value_name='temp'); long = long.sort_values('city', kind='stable').reset_index(drop=True); return long[['city', 'month', 'temp']] — the stable sort keeps the calendar order that melt gave each city's months."
        ],
        "reasoning": "<p>Melt is a shape translation, not a computation: the number of facts is invariant (cities × months in, same count out), and each wide header becomes a value in a <code>month</code> column. The ordering is the subtle part — melt emits <b>month-major</b> blocks (every city's January first), while the spec asks for <b>city-major</b> rows. A <i>stable</i> sort by city fixes exactly this: it regroups by city while preserving the calendar order each city's months already had inside the melt blocks. Unstable sorts would also regroup but could scramble the months within a city — determinism is not just about which key sorts, but how.</p>",
        "solution": "import pandas as pd\n\ndef to_long(wide):\n    \"\"\"Melt the wide monthly table into long form.\"\"\"\n    long = wide.melt(id_vars=\"city\", var_name=\"month\", value_name=\"temp\")\n    long = long.sort_values(\"city\", kind=\"stable\").reset_index(drop=True)\n    return long[[\"city\", \"month\", \"temp\"]]\n"
      }
