        "hints": [
          "Each grid cell answers 'how many tickets did this team log on this day?' — that is a group-by-two-keys sum. Which single call groups by two keys AND lays one of them out as columns?",
          "pivot_table(index='team', columns='day', values='tickets', aggfunc='sum', fill_value=0) builds the grid; rows come out sorted by team and columns sorted alphabetically — which for Mon/Tue/Wed is exactly the calendar order.",
          "grid = batches.pivot_table(index='team', columns='day', values='tickets', aggfunc='sum', fill_value=0); return grid[['Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed']].reset_index() — selecting the day list pins the column order, reset_index turns team back into a column."
        ],
        "reasoning": "<p>A grid is a two-key aggregation displayed as a rectangle: each cell is \"sum of tickets for (team, day)\" — groupby semantics — and the reshape merely bends the <code>day</code> key into columns. <code>pivot_table</code> does both at once, and the details are where the meaning lives: <code>aggfunc=\"sum\"</code> says duplicate batches <b>add up</b> rather than collide, and <code>fill_value=0</code> is a semantic claim — an unobserved team-day had zero tickets logged, which is true for counts but would be a lie for, say, an average. The sorted index and columns are pivot_table's contract, which is what lets the spec promise \"teams ascending, days in order\".</p>",
        "solution": "import pandas as pd\n\ndef team_grid(batches):\n    \"\"\"Return the team-by-day ticket grid with 0 for gaps.\"\"\"\n    grid = batches.pivot_table(\n        index=\"team\", columns=\"day\", values=\"tickets\",\n        aggfunc=\"sum\", fill_value=0,\n    )\n    return grid[[\"Mon\", \"Tue\", \"Wed\"]].reset_index()\n"
      }
