        "hints": [
          "If the same (region, quarter) appears twice, what happens if you reshape first and aggregate later? (.pivot raises on duplicate key pairs — why?)",
          "Aggregate first: groupby(['region', 'quarter'])['sales'].sum() produces one value per pair. Then .unstack('quarter') bends the quarter level of the index into columns — and leaves region as the row index.",
          "agg = sales_long.groupby(['region', 'quarter'])['sales'].sum(); wide = agg.unstack('quarter').fillna(0); return wide[['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4']].reset_index() — the column selection pins Q1..Q4 order and reset_index names the region column."
        ],
        "reasoning": "<p>Duplicate keys force the order of operations: reshaping a long table with repeated (region, quarter) pairs is ill-defined — which value should the cell hold? — so <code>.pivot</code> refuses. Aggregating <b>first</b> resolves the ambiguity (sum, per the spec) and yields a Series with a two-level index holding exactly one value per pair; <code>unstack</code> is then purely mechanical, moving the quarter level into columns. The remaining gaps — region-quarters that never occurred — surface as NaN after the unstack, and <code>fillna(0)</code> states what absence means for a sales total. Aggregate, then reshape: the safest ordering whenever duplicates are possible.</p>",
        "solution": "import pandas as pd\n\ndef quarterly_matrix(sales_long):\n    \"\"\"Return the region-by-quarter summed sales matrix.\"\"\"\n    agg = sales_long.groupby([\"region\", \"quarter\"])[\"sales\"].sum()\n    wide = agg.unstack(\"quarter\").fillna(0)\n    return wide[[\"Q1\", \"Q2\", \"Q3\", \"Q4\"]].reset_index()\n"
      }
